--><rss xmlns:content="http://purl.org/rss/1.0/modules/content/" xmlns:wfw="http://wellformedweb.org/CommentAPI/" xmlns:itunes="http://www.itunes.com/dtds/podcast-1.0.dtd" xmlns:dc="http://purl.org/dc/elements/1.1/" xmlns:media="http://www.rssboard.org/media-rss" version="2.0"><channel><title>Blog - NODNAL</title><link>https://www.nodnal.com/blog/</link><lastBuildDate>Tue, 06 Oct 2026 05:00:50 +0000</lastBuildDate><language>en-US</language><generator>Site-Server v@build.version@ (http://www.squarespace.com)</generator><description><![CDATA[]]></description><item><title>French Direct</title><dc:creator>Landon Kinsey</dc:creator><pubDate>Wed, 30 Sep 2026 22:10:07 +0000</pubDate><link>https://www.nodnal.com/blog/french-direct</link><guid isPermaLink="false">642b125d971c00429eb73c10:66c78d033aeb0942f42d0dce:6abc373fb6387b2b14fa2077</guid><description><![CDATA[<p data-rte-preserve-empty="true"></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><strong>“I’m isolated from those who are isolated from this”</strong>… a friend&nbsp;</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true"></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true"></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true"></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><strong>Climber Jargon</strong></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><strong>beta</strong>-  shared knowledge of route, conditions, approach, anything relevant one climber can share with the next</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><strong>rappel</strong>-  descending ropes </p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><strong>abalakov or v-thread</strong>-  a technique to rappel on ice where two holes making a V are drilled and the rope is run through them to rappel on</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><strong>v-thread tool</strong>-  A little device used to snare or hook the end of the rope through the V</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><strong>névé</strong>- young, granular type of snow that has been partially melted, refrozen, and compacted</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><strong>protection/ ‘pro’- </strong>anything used to protect a climb;  could be pitons, bolts, nuts, cams, beaks, natural pro, etc. for rock; screws are used to protect ice and pickets are used to protect in snow</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><strong>french aid- </strong>a slang term used to describe when a climber pulls on a piece of pro to help them through the movement</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><strong>penitentes</strong>- are spectacular, blade-like snow and ice formations found in higher altitudes</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><strong>bergschrund<em>/ </em>‘schrund’ or <em>rimaya</em></strong>- a deep crevasse where the glacier and face of the mountain (fixed rock headwall or ice) separate</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true"></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal"><strong>&nbsp;</strong></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal"></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal"></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal"><strong>Leaving and Preparation</strong></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">I made the decision to come to Peru just last week.  A few hours north of where I live in central Ecuador, friends were rock climbing and had invited me along.   After preparing all my things to camp and climb, purchasing the bus ticket at the terminal, and arranging a stay for Robin, I stood outside my door with my bags by my feet and became overwhelmed by the imminent five hours of bus travel on high, windy road.&nbsp; I don’t know why but in recent years, my anxiety around bus travel and transit in general has progressively limited my travel and exploration.  My sensitivity to motion sickness, something I have struggled with most of my life, seems to be worsening.&nbsp; All it takes is a thought of the bus and the nausea enters my body and my head begins to spin.  With ticket in hand for the late night bus, I re-entered my apartment and instead of leaving for an unexplored region of north, I instead found myself in my bed, defeated.  The next day, because I had a few days of a clear schedule I decided to spend it in nearby Cajas National Park, the closest wilderness access from my apartment.&nbsp; I hiked around and spent a couple nights sleeping at around 12,500ft.&nbsp;&nbsp;While there, I decided to buy my $200 flight to Lima and as soon as I did, an interesting idea popped into my head. </p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">Leaving is always interesting.&nbsp; Getting Robin squared away with friends-&nbsp; expressing my gratitude to the family that sees after her by cooking a large pot of chili from scratch, roasting and grinding the coriander seeds to make the chili powder.&nbsp; It’s my girlfriend’s birthday and we were out celebrating with friends.&nbsp; We are drinking and playing games in a warm lit and cozy restaurant, and my mind is elsewhere- from getting laundry hung up so it’s dry by the next morning to deciding on which rock and ice protection I’ll bring on this trip.  I stand up and give my early goodbyes for the evening.  I finishing packing my duffels and my girlfriend comes over later to spend the night, leaving the next morning.&nbsp; I look around and realize no one else is in the apartment complex, as if to give me room to leave quietly and clean.&nbsp;</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">Yesterday at 1230p I took an Uber to the mini-bus station.&nbsp; Then went on a four hour bus ride on high curvy roads descending toward the coast.&nbsp; Having done yoga asanas that morning specifically for motion sickness I think “wow, it might be helping, I only feel mildly sick”.&nbsp; I also took a Gabapentin.&nbsp; Disassociation helps.&nbsp;Hauling my heavy bags through the airport I arrive at check-in to unexpected costs and afterward step outside to enjoy a moment of the warm, thick air of the coast.&nbsp;At 11p I am in Peru, after a three hour plane ride to Lima.  An hour of lines and clearing immigration, recovering bags- noise, stimulation and stress.&nbsp; I taxi to a pre-booked hostel after negotiating with several drivers learning later that I wasn’t being taxed to hard for my white skin; the new Lima airport charges an outrageous amount for them to do business there, so all costs are driven up from what I recollect from previous years.&nbsp; After nearly an hour to go only a few miles which is the hell of Lima traffic I check in to my hostel and am quickly asleep at 1a.&nbsp; Just as fast, I am up and eating a breakfast of an egg and bread and then Uber to the airport to board for my hour flight to Carhuaz.&nbsp; At baggage claim the taxi drivers accost me to buy a ride and I agree to one as long as I can secure the front seat and after an hour of drive am kindly dropped off at a hostel I am well acquainted with in Huaraz.&nbsp; Within minutes I am renting snow stakes and buying fuel.&nbsp; I rest for a moment to give my spine a break; then am off to town to get cash and supplies- bulks of food from the market, sunscreen that will actually protect my skin, little things, all the while meal-planning in my head.&nbsp; I return to the hostel to draw beta from other climbers about what they have encountered in the mountains this season and what they can tell me about conditions.&nbsp; I ask specific questions and this quickly filters out who to spend my time relating over these things with.&nbsp; One Chilean guide was generous in showing me some photos from the past few excursions in the mountains and it struck me how dry the range looked this year.  The marijuana is burning around me and although tempting I’ve vowed to do what might be the hardest thing for a climber in Huaraz-&nbsp; not smoke.&nbsp;&nbsp; I went out for lunch to my favorite spot and had a <em>causa ceviche, </em>later enjoying a beer while socializing a bit and waiting for the evening energy to calm down.</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">Two days of transit and prep and now it’s time for bed.&nbsp;</p>


  



































  
    
  

    

    

    
