  <p data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><strong>September 1</strong></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">Yet another full-on, focused on the task at hand, day.&nbsp; I got out of bed at 9a feeling restored after so much sitting and anxious walking the two days previous..&nbsp; Pretty quickly I went into town searching for a sleeping pad.&nbsp; I oddly found the exact pad I was going to order from the US when I returned from this trip, so without a thought I purchased it on the spot.  I got back from the center and made a quick lunch.&nbsp; Yesterday I met a European guy who had a successful guided climb on the idea that popped into my head.&nbsp; We talked in length about the glacier and the condition of the Bergschrund.  I asked him what his logisitics looked like for him to make a successful climb.  He said he paid about 1,800 Euro for the six-day trip and had a guide, a cook, four porters and many burros.  </p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">I left Huaraz at 1230p and took a <em>combi,</em> a shared public mini-bus. It took us three hours to reach Caraz with the hundred stops we made.&nbsp; I transferred into a <em>moto-taxi</em> and then to a <em>pasajero </em>(similar to a <em>combi</em>).&nbsp; We ascended for 90 minutes to the small village of Cachapampa where I set-up my tent at sundown.  My host has cuy on the menu and while searching for the shower I found their cages.&nbsp;From where I am now writing inside my tent on the lawn of this hostel, I will need to gain 3,000 meters/ 10,000 feet to the peak.  The trailhead is right across the dirt road 50 feet away.</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal"><strong>&nbsp;</strong></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal"><strong>Approach</strong>&nbsp;</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">I was up last night for a few hours listening to dogs growl and bark at I don’t know what.&nbsp; The energy from the sky and moon felt strong and I was restless, until passing into a deep sleep.&nbsp; I awoke later than wanted, but naturally.&nbsp; This native village I am in is typical, peaceful moments of birds chirping with intermittent spurts of loud music from houses and cars or some kind of animal drama.&nbsp; My host is running around from one doing to the next and each need (drinking water, outlet, shower, toilet, <em>arriero</em>) I express is forgotten.&nbsp; Before leaving she apologized for being so neglectful and assured me that when I return she’d be more available and we had a friendly goodbye.</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">When the burro driver, the <em>arriero</em>, returned with his burros I asked him to sit down and offered him some bread and coffee.&nbsp; I didn’t have enough cash to pay for both him and the night of camping so instead of hiring him for two days of burro support, I negotiated a day and a morning with him.&nbsp; He would take two burros and we would manage about 2/3 of the way to Base Camp, so I would have his help for about 10 miles carrying my climbing equipment and sleeping system.</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">I carried my climbing day-pack for about 8 miles and took it easy being that the trail is very gradual and fool proof.&nbsp; I found my <em>arriero</em> waiting by a rock and he proposed we stop there, proclaiming his burros needed to graze for the evening.&nbsp; I cooked us both a delicious pasta over a fire I built, puttingsweet potato in the coals for the next morning.&nbsp;We’re camped at 3,866m/ 12,700ft and I am sleeping open air.&nbsp;</p>


  



































  
    
  

    

    

    
