  <p data-rte-preserve-empty="true">A couple of days have elapsed since I last wrote. From two nights ago, sleeping open air, a rain scare forced me in the middle of the night to pitch my tent shell.&nbsp;I’m carrying the outer wall to my mountaineering tent; it’s lightweight and strong.&nbsp;The <em>arriero</em> built a fire in the morning and I cooked us some oats over it.&nbsp; He packed up the burros Colossal and Jorge.&nbsp;We walked and talked and he taught me some burro commands in Quechua, which are oddly similar to some of the noises I command Robin my dog with.&nbsp;We passed the lake and arrived to a big open tundra.&nbsp;A light storm was approaching with precipitation.&nbsp;After unloading the burros we shook hands and he departed.&nbsp;I then creatively loaded my pack which I am guessing is ~75 pounds by how it feels loading my mid-back.  I labored hard as the trail begins switch-backing to base camp.&nbsp; But after two hours with a short break in between I arrived to base camp at 4,300 meters/ 14,100 feet.&nbsp; I cleared a spot for my tent, cooked lunch and napped an hour.&nbsp; Then I reloaded my pack with my climbing equipment.&nbsp; I ascended to a higher camp on the moraine, the edge of the glacier, stashing my gear in some rocks.&nbsp; I returned damp, cooked and stretched and immediately passed out.  </p>


  



































  
    
  

    

    

    
