  <p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">This morning I naturally awoke early, physically prepared and ready to move on, but mentally wise.&nbsp; I laid not rising until 9a.&nbsp; I’ve been battling a sore throat and light cold since the beginning of this trip and was happy to have a reduction of symptoms this morning as well as a reduction of pain in my knee.&nbsp; On the first day of hiking, I deviated off trail and not paying attention stupidly ran into a cactus.&nbsp; I was in shorts and the two needles in my knee looked like nature’s version of an acupuncture treatment.&nbsp; But those needles would not come out easy, my skin raised an inch as I ripped them out!&nbsp; So for the last two days I have been with this knee pain and experiencing relief today.  For the rapid acclimatization I am undergoing (not recommended), I am religious with my hydration.&nbsp; I also rely a lot on a local herb called <em>muna</em>, AKA Andean mint, which is great for the altitude and soft on the stomach.&nbsp; And cocaine, lots of cocaine, if you want to know my secrets.&nbsp; </p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">I left the tent and entered beautiful sunshine.&nbsp; I read some John Muir, a harrowing tale about a dog and him traversing a vast glacier.&nbsp; I prepared a welcomed meal of oats with fruit and stretched for an hour.&nbsp; My body was feeling great, well balanced and restored.&nbsp; I stashed food in the trees and even sat on a high limb and played my flute in the forest for a while.&nbsp; After de-camping I said goodbye the greenery and slowly made my way back up the moraine, arriving after a brisk 90 minutes of stout walking enjoying fresh glacial melt along the way.&nbsp; I arrived at my stashed gear and found a perfectly flat, wind protected spot to camp.&nbsp; I setup in the dirt, prepared some pasta and got into mountaineering mode around 4p.&nbsp; I ascended the glacier passing over several crevasses finally reaching a place to stash some equipment&nbsp; What took me 75 minutes to ascend I descended in 12.&nbsp; I arrived back to my camp on the moraine at dark:&nbsp; 5,000 meters/ 16,300 feet.&nbsp; Since then I have been pounding fluids, and hearing big rockfall and avalanche in the nearby amphitheater.&nbsp; Post sunset is usually when mountains are expected to quiet as everything begins to freeze.&nbsp; As I laid there enjoying the brilliant night sky listening to the symphony of very large features ripping down the mountains, I contemplated this.</p>


  










  





















  











  
    
      
    
  
  
    
      
        
          
        
      
    
    
    



  








  
  
  




  
  
  
  
    
  


  
  <p data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><strong>Glacier Approach</strong></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">I departed the moraine at 715a this morning, expecting a quick ascent to high camp.&nbsp; Having navigated most of it last evening (to the base of the technical section) I felt good under the weight of my pack- until I decided to haul it.&nbsp; The first haul went totally fine but during I got a bit disoriented by what was overhead.&nbsp; I drifted a bit left and instead of continuing, I climbed directly up a few meters of vertical to overhanging solid névé using a bit of french aid.&nbsp; Albeit having solid feet and sticky tool placements, a fall would have been consequential. After clearing the lip I found ice high and left to build a belay and from there began hauling my pack on a micro-traxion.&nbsp; I’m not an aid or big-wall climber and this really showed once I realized my pack wouldn’t clear the lip. Taught and at a diagnol, I could tension my only 70m, 7.1mm rope to exactly half.&nbsp; That was the beginning of a two-hour ordeal of failure after failure trying to free my pack.&nbsp; I descended and re-ascended multiple times in a kind of fury.&nbsp; Eventually, after deciding to fix the rope, descend on it and go off the end to make a traverse into the pack, I freed it; but, to get to it I had to crawl and scooch on my butt.&nbsp; When I got back to the anchor I checked my harness and my heart sank in my chest.&nbsp; My coveted and much needed v-thread tool was missing.&nbsp; Before I had descended I had removed it and clipped it to a back loop of my newer and less familiar harness knowing better once I did it, but did it anyway.&nbsp; Realizing now my v-thread tool is gone I thought “this will be my turning point”.&nbsp; I can’t go any further without the ability to drill rappels.&nbsp;</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">I mustered the will to descend and give things a look, curious if it had stuck into the slope somewhere below.&nbsp; My guess was that it dislodged as I was crawling and scooching, so I descended 40 meters downclimbing and traversed.&nbsp; No tool!&nbsp; My head slung.&nbsp; But when I turned around there it was, shining bright yellow in the snow and easily retrievable.&nbsp; I zipped it up in my jacket thanking the situation and was on my way!</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">I ascended the rope and gathered myself.&nbsp; To the climbers right was a rap line but from below, in a field of <em>penitentes</em>, was not what looked like the way up.&nbsp; To the left were some old signs of a track in the snow and it looked like it relieved in angle above.  This was another fuck-up, well two.&nbsp; I am following other footsteps blindly and even worse, referencing Gaia to tell me where the saddle is.&nbsp; As I booted up I felt so silly.&nbsp; Here I was for two hours undergoing all sorts of silly things right beneath the point at which this glacier tops out..  Only to discover the footsteps I had followed disappeared into a massive, rotted out snow-bridge.&nbsp; This crevasse was horrific and eternal.&nbsp; I stomped a ledge and donned my pack, traversing along it’s corniced lip only to discover a dead-end in front me, another horrific gap.&nbsp; But, in the distance I got my first glimpse of the mountain I came to climb but have never actually seen until now- Alpamayo.</p>


  



































  
    
  

    

    

    
