  <p data-rte-preserve-empty="true">The air to my back registered as I sat on this perch staring into the face.&nbsp; I then comfortably down-climbed back to my little ice belay cave.&nbsp; Traversing past it a few meters I saw a distinct maze of footsteps, gained the “technical push” climbing it quite securely and unlabored even with the pack on.&nbsp; I was now at high camp and at 5,500 meters/ 18,000 feet.  I arrived to three tents, with three Peruanos hanging out as cooks and porters.&nbsp; Three more people, all Swiss, were descending Quitaraju, the neighboring peak. I setup camp in the snow, made water, and after an hour of resting was awoken by the Swiss couple and guide, at 4p.&nbsp; They had left camp at 2a.&nbsp; The guide, who also lives in Ecuador, took a liking to what I was doing.&nbsp; He said he just had one of the longest days of guiding, after managing a descent as long as the ascent, needing to make all sorts of ugly, messy rappels through <em>penitentes</em>.&nbsp; He then re-affirmed, because of it’s solar aspect, that the conditions on the face of Alpamayo should be much more favorable.&nbsp; He’s been on it five times in years previous since the early 90’s.&nbsp;</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">Now up, I got booted and suited and went for a reconnaissance hike on the upper glacier, making mental photos of the features around the bergschrund, the entrance to the actual face and the route I am interested in climbing called the French Direct. </p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">As I now lay here in the tent, with a layer of frost forming on everything around, climbing that ‘schrund felt a bit ominous.&nbsp; “I’m curious what ice conditions I will find?”&nbsp; Tomorrow will be a very long day, and if done calmly and steadily, a memorable one.&nbsp;</p>


  



































  
    
  

    

    

    
