  <p data-rte-preserve-empty="true"><strong>The Climb</strong></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Quite inspired by Muir’s writings!&nbsp; I just read how he managed his way across a remote glacier in Alaska dragging, hauling and pushing 100 pounds of sled over treacherous snow bridges in heavily crevassed terrain.&nbsp;His feet are day by day becoming colder and wetter because his boots are failing.&nbsp; He's become nearly snowblind and is seeing double and is days from where people are camped with a vast expanse of crevasses in between. From his writing he's apparently loving every minute of it!&nbsp; Experiencing the raw fragilities of nature, make-shifting a small fire to brew tea on his chest as he lays in his bear-skin bag on an icy abyss.&nbsp; “Nearly blind” he is healing his eyes by covering them with wet bandages and remarks how a hummingbird came to visit him on the glacier, twice.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true">It's dark now and a warm fire is to my back.&nbsp; It took me a while to get the fire going despite the abundance of dead, remarkably dry material around.&nbsp; Initially I gathered a bunch of dry grass and lit it up.&nbsp; Building this elaborate structure around it the fire looked beautiful and impressive… and quickly died out.&nbsp; I tried again and again but moved with a sort of conceit saying to myself "it will light”.&nbsp; &nbsp;Then I slowed down.&nbsp; And realized I can slow down.&nbsp; I gathered some interesting dead material and taking a bit of it I got it to go as tinder. &nbsp;On my knees bit by bit I built it up with substantial warmth to which now I am enjoying. Fire doesn't reward complacency.&nbsp; I just watched a moth go into the flame.</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true">This morning I awoke to swollen eyes and blurry vision.&nbsp; My eyelids and eyebrows are burnt.&nbsp;&nbsp;Muir was suffering three months of bronchitis which magically disappeared during his glacial pursuit.&nbsp; I seem to have contracted some kind of sinus infection, discharging radical amounts of mucus.&nbsp; I've had a horrid cough with chest pains for the past few days; but that seems to be bettering as I am now camped in a sub-alpine forest surrounded by sounds of running water and greenery.&nbsp; I rested very well.&nbsp; My day has been remarked by no physical activity, eating, drinking and enjoying the birds that have come to visit me.&nbsp;</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Yesterday morning around 2a I was wide-eyed inside my bag.&nbsp; My pad was directly on the snow and I could feel body heat vacating through whatever side pressured the ground. Surprisingly though I was warm enough to enjoy the night.&nbsp; My pad is brilliant!&nbsp; And my bag is an old reliable friend.&nbsp; I could only sleep from 9p to 10p, one hour.&nbsp; As each hour passed by and I heard the ‘ting’ from my watch, I realized that for the past 70 hours I had only slept in three, one hour increments with a handful of 10 to 15 minute naps.&nbsp; Nerve strain.</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Peeking outside the tent I couldn't see more than a couple meters.&nbsp; Camp was completely fogged in, but I decided that although I am an hour before my 330a alarm that I would start prepping and beginning what would be one massive day!&nbsp; I had packed some fresh snow the evening before which I began melting to hydrate with.&nbsp; All of my stuff was at the foot of my sleeping bag including my fuel canister to conserve efficiency. I started layering deciding if I should wear thermals or not (I’m glad I did), drinking green tea and <em>muna</em>.&nbsp; I was&nbsp;deciding exactly what I want to bring to climb for the day, with each locking carabiner and sling and setup carefully decided on to minimize decisions.&nbsp; For instance knowing my one green locker goes with my prusik cord is the same color combo as my belay plate.&nbsp; I don't like mix matching equipment; that drains energy when stressed.&nbsp; After getting my harness organized I exited my sleeping bag and put on my boots.  This slow, quiet and methodical transition felt peaceful.&nbsp; </p>


  



































  
    
  

    

    

    
