  <p data-rte-preserve-empty="true">I left camp 340a and descended into a glacier basin to approach the wall.&nbsp; It was foggy and cold as I moved slow stepping over a few narrow crevasses.&nbsp; I was surprised to find a tricky section at the base of the snow slope.&nbsp; Perched at the edge of a crevasse I had to fall into the snow and use my knee to gain the slope trusting my axe placement and using a trekking pole horizontally stuck in as leverage.&nbsp; Down to the left was just black.&nbsp; Down to the right was a snow filled ledge about 20 feet angled down; still I did not want to fall.&nbsp; Well ascending the slope is was no clear footpath. Despite it being a popular route I was the only one there and some storms had passed through since the last party of Russians descended nearly a week earlier. But despite the snowfall and drift there was not enough accumulation to concern me about wind slab.&nbsp; I could faintly see depressions in the snow by some foot travel and also lines caused by ice and snow fall.&nbsp; Using common sense I followed the lines that had bends in them.</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true">At the top of the steep snow slope I shined my light around and saw the bergschrund. &nbsp; Getting into it is tricky and it’s weighed on my mind for a week now.&nbsp; I saw what looked like the rappel line.&nbsp; So I climbed this little snow dome of part consolidated, part dry loose that was steep and exposed.&nbsp; When I crawled on top I discovered it was indeed the rappel line.&nbsp; The bergschrund entry was above me to the left- I found it!&nbsp; From this angle, the climbing into the route looked very accessible, easier than expected.&nbsp; I looked down to the left and noticed a stomped out platform in the snow so I descended to it. It was now 5a and I saw a headlamp from the distant high camp.&nbsp; I flaked the rope out of my pack, adjusted some things, turned off my light and sat quiet in the dark.&nbsp; It was very calm.</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true">I did a humming exercise to give my nervous system a little support.&nbsp; As I started to shiver, I tied in as if I was on belay and started into the ‘schrund.&nbsp; There's basically a snow bridge under some low angle ice blown into scattered ice features below.&nbsp; The first twenty feet of climbing was easy and then it reached into this shelf.&nbsp; I couldn't tell if I should go left or right, but I decided to go right, even though steeper because that's where the rappel lines were which means anchor. It became overhanging and I started to feel insecure. &nbsp;I crawled underneath the shelf and after clearing away some fragile garbage, positioned myself into a spot I could place a screw.&nbsp; I got a screw in and went in direct.&nbsp; I then placed another closer to the bulge I needed to maneuver over.&nbsp; Everywhere I swung it broke.&nbsp; Snice!&nbsp; I would scratch my tools and they would just fall out not hooking on anything just cutting through.&nbsp; I would stomp once and it would break.&nbsp; I then swung my tool around up to the right and into some firm névé. My tool was at a weird angle.&nbsp; I decided I could manage this crux but did not want the consequential risk so I placed a stubby screw and girth hitched it to a double runner.&nbsp; I got both tools in a weird angle and actually found a hand jam in the ice at my hip to leverage off of.&nbsp; I humped my right knee over and with my left leg hanging was able to get some front points in the actual runnel beneath my tools.&nbsp; I cleared what was in front of me and got in another screw, leaned down and around and unclipped the double runner, leaving it behind (I would return later for on descent).&nbsp; After 90 minutes navigating this short section, I made it to the first anchor of material running through some ice, clipped in and looked upward into a foggy runnel of mystery.&nbsp;</p>


  










  





















  











  
    
      
    
  
  
    
      
        
          
        
      
    
    
    



  








  
  
  




  
  
  
  
    
  


  
  <p data-rte-preserve-empty="true">At this point I donned my headlamp, flaked the rope into my pack, and began the ritual of slowly front-pointing and swinging for good placements.&nbsp; My lungs were labored and my calves burned but as I got into the rhythm I began to much enjoy what I was undertaking.&nbsp; I picked this objective for this reason, to get into a rhythm of climbing.&nbsp; The route looked, and felt, virgin!&nbsp; Every tool and foot I had to find, which felt so pure and wonderful.&nbsp; I was fogged in so when I looked down I saw nothing and when I looked up I saw nothing but what was right in front of me-&nbsp; a mix of super secure névé and hard, alpine ice.&nbsp; The route goes at a consistent AI3 grade with a few steeper sections.&nbsp; Half-way up the couloir the fog and spindrift eased and I got a 15 minute window viewing high camp.&nbsp; Seeing the other party gathered I went direct into a screw and waved (without binoculars I’m but a speck on the face but nonetheless they knew I was on route, I later learned).&nbsp; About 2/3 of the way up there was some rocks I decided to climb through using ice all the way.&nbsp; I looked down and around and felt this incredible joy inside!&nbsp; The climbing was so good and as I climbed it just got better and better.&nbsp; I stopped gripping and kicking so hard.&nbsp; The less I struggled, the safer I felt.</p>


  










  





















  











  
    
      
    
  
  
    
      
        
          
        
      
    
    
    



  








  
  
  




  
  
  
  
    
  


  
  <p data-rte-preserve-empty="true">I continued anchor to anchor, checking their integrity and making mental notes for later descent.&nbsp; Some were good, others suspect.&nbsp; Sometimes I would just place a screw and rest. The last bit of the actual climbing line was kicked and picked out. The climbing was easy.&nbsp; The last 50 meters steepened into this fun, stemmy section.&nbsp; At 9a I topped the line with a massive smile, arriving at a fixed snow stake on the summit ridge at 5940 meters/ 19,500 feet.&nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true">Conditions were poor.&nbsp; It was foggy and windy, not quite storming.&nbsp; I hammered in a stake on the summit ridge and tried doubling my rope through a prusik to protect myself. I walked about 20 meters on the knife edge and saw my rope dangling below to the right.&nbsp; I tugged gently and realized it was hooked on something below.&nbsp; Not wanting to compound the first warning sign I double backed and untied figuring I would deal with it after the summit.&nbsp; Placing one foot in front of the other, I walked further on the exposed ridge arriving to a dramatic corniced section.&nbsp; Probably 50 at most 100 feet from what I believe was the summit, I shook my head and said “no”.&nbsp; In that moment I cautiously knelt on a knee and felt so much accomplishment overwhelm me.&nbsp; I turned my back to the objective.&nbsp; I would not summit.</p>


  



































  
    
  

    

    

    
