  <p data-rte-preserve-empty="true">At 2p I crested over the saddle toward the lower glacier. It was a bit complicated getting down with my one rope. Anchors were setup for two. I found three anchors which I rapped, making two intermediate rappels on threads, with a pack lower somewhere in there. I reached the moraine two hours later, utterly exhausted. I had some food stashed and made a delicious pasta which disappeared in front of eyes I don’t know where it went. It was remarkable how everything I did from arrival to high camp through the moraine was so effortless and without a conscious will. I descended in twighlight, reaching basecamp at dark. I poked my head into the big cook tent from the Swiss expedition and was greeted by half a dozen local guys and in the middle was B, the Swiss guide, smiling wide.</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">I asked if he could relay a message to my girlfriend, to let her know that I was safe and enjoying the solitude in the mountains and pretty scenery. He said they had wifi. “Damn Swiss”, I thought. I connected, was flooded with 10 days of Whatsapp messages, but only sending one message off to my girlfriend.&nbsp; I quickly disconnected my phone.  I then thought “wow, people care about me”.  I started thinking of all the ways I am cared for and how I care for others.&nbsp;  I reflected on how I recently led this yoga event and how each person, one by one, approached me afterward and in detail described how this afternoon event had touched them.&nbsp; How in the past 6 months I have watched my coaching client undergo these incredible shifts in life perspective, transformative, lasting changes for him.&nbsp; How my partner has asked me if I can feel the love she gives me with me often blanking, and yet at times increasingly glimpsing past the fear into the feelings she talks about.</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">I was invited to dinner, a three course meal with the Swiss.&nbsp; A tall beer, soup, rice and veggie and beef plate with delicious fruit.&nbsp; The Swiss were generous, and in Swiss fashion.&nbsp; The guide asked if I was more of rock climber or a mountaineer and I responded that I like mountains, and that at heart I am skiier.&nbsp; Before leaving the Swiss woman told me she saw me up the face before the clouds covered me.&nbsp; She wasn’t very logical with what she was saying but she didn’t need to be because I understood exactly what she was saying as she teared up.&nbsp; “Take care of yourself”, she said.</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">The truth is, I am not here to discard my body or off to do the next hardman thing.&nbsp; I am out here to feel.&nbsp; Every day I have grieved, and moreso now the tears are welcomed on my dry skin.  I’m back to the fire now and will enjoy a tune on my flute before sleep.</p>


  



































  
    
  

    

    

    
