  <p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal"><br><strong>Death</strong></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">&nbsp;It is now thrusday night and I am laying on a bed, in Caraz (the nearest town below the village that accesses the valley/<em>quebrada</em>).&nbsp; A few hours ago I arrived and took my first shower in 10 days.&nbsp; I have been shoveling in food.&nbsp; I took 1g of motrin but it didn’t effect me seemingly at all.&nbsp; My mood is undergoing intense swings; one moment I’ll feel really happy and then calm, 30 minutes later I will feel angry, and then minutes later will be overwhelmed by a volatile discharge of grief.&nbsp; My body feels warm, and my personal interactions with people have been strikingly light and friendly.  </p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">I last wrote by the fire three days ago and after I set my pencil down I thought that might be the last I would write about this solo adventure. &nbsp;I covered the fire with dry cow patties, a trick to hold in the embers with the intent to cook over it in the morning. But the mountains had other plans.&nbsp;I woke at basecamp to an intense freezing rain with a dramatic temperature drop.&nbsp; After a window of partial clearing I summoned myself to go retrieve my stored climbing gear I left on the moraine.&nbsp; Whilst coming down from the climb two days ago, I didn’t have the energy to consolidate everything into my pack so I stashed it up there. After being tent bound all morning by dreary conditions when I finally arose I realized my wrist was hurting something terrible.&nbsp; My left hand was contracted as well as my forearm. &nbsp;Anyhow, with a labored breath I made my way back up to the moraine, much slower than before.&nbsp; It was quite challenging packing my pack with all my climbing equipment- rope, harness, 5x lockers, 3x slings, 3x screws, 3x ice tools, 2x snow pickets, crampons, boots, and a few misc items.&nbsp; I did this in a mix of hail and freezing rain and snow, but managed just fine and returned to my camp in the dark.  After removing all my wet clothes I went to bed.&nbsp;</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">The next morning just about everything was wet, and like always I did my best to protect my down bag and puffy.&nbsp; My sinuses were slowly improving but was still under some head pressure and expelling mucus every 10 minutes.&nbsp; My throat continued to be an annoyance, as it had for the previous eight days.&nbsp; Decamping was a laborious task, using my teeth instead of my left hand and had it took great creativity to get everything into/onto my pack.&nbsp; The mix of low pressure and cold was amplifying the pain in forearm and at moments I realized I was tearing up from the pain.&nbsp; I had been running out of food and was down to cheesy, buttery, polenta which I happily discovered is an excellent backcountry meal. “How am I just discovering this? This is amazing!”</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">I started descending from base camp at 830a and after two hours of walking under load I was around the same place that the <em>arriero</em> left me on the second morning when I heard someone yelling. I turned around to a guy running toward me and as he approached I realized he was a climber.&nbsp; With piercing eyes, T said that he needed service immediately, sharing that he just watched his friend disappear while paragliding&nbsp;</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">Being that I was mostly tentbound the two days prior, and camped a 15 minute walk from the standard basecamp where the climber trail passes through, I had missed them.&nbsp; I thought I was the only one out there, but apparently these two Frenchmen had ascended rather quickly with the intent to climb the same route I had just come from.&nbsp; Waking early at high camp and realizing conditions were too poor to climb, they decided to launch from the saddle on paraglides in poor conditions.&nbsp; P went first, T launched just behind him.&nbsp; He watched as a gust of wind came and propelled his friend hard and left into a series of buttresses protruding through the glacier and this is where his friend then disappeared.&nbsp;</p>


  










  





















  











  
    
      
    
  
  
    
      
        
          
        
      
    
    
    



  








  
  
  




  
  
  
  
    
  


  
  <p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">&nbsp;</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">T landed near base camp and in this time his friend sent out a series of rescue calls from his Garmin.&nbsp; Although T received confirmation that P made these calls, his Garmin was not contacting rescue.&nbsp; This is what I was able to gather from him, while we stood there in a fit of uncertainty together, wondering what our next moves should be.  As I stood there with him, two things occurred to me.&nbsp; The first was the implausibility of a helicopter coming.&nbsp; Conditions were still very poor.&nbsp; The second thing that came was the fact that his friend had made rescue calls.&nbsp; This implied that he was alive upon impact, which implies he may still be alive up there, and if so, in need of help.</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">T was compelled downward, to get phone signal and call in a helicopter.&nbsp; I walked with him for a bit, reminding him of what is present to which he welcomed.&nbsp; We came to an <em>arriero</em> with a handful of burros and the man said that the first camp (another 3-4 hours hike down) has wifi.&nbsp; I felt something pulling me back up and told T that I felt a strong urge to go search.  So I dumped everything unneeded into a cave and with warm clothes, sleeping bag and pad, and medical supply I started to ascend.&nbsp; I then crossed a party of three french hikers on the trail who fed me.&nbsp; Before I departed one of the men leaned and said to me “it is courageous what you are doing, and kind”.  That acknowledgement fueled me.  </p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">I ascended from there past base camp and into the moraine, in a storm. I was moved by a calm swiftness I cannot describe.&nbsp; Aware of the very small chance P is alive, he will be desperate. Many practical questions passed through my mind as I traversed the edge of the glacier looking for an orange pack and French flag chute.&nbsp; At some point, an interesting feeling entered me and a suspicious thought: “did P fly off”?&nbsp; I oddly began to feel light and smile.&nbsp; I then presumed calling his name, traversing and praying.&nbsp; This went on for about five hours.&nbsp;</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">As light was fading and without any signs of him, I decided I must go down.&nbsp; A headlamp wouldn’t signal in this weather.&nbsp; The glacier was falling apart in the afternoon warmth and I was venturing into areas around the glacier that pushed my edge of comfort.&nbsp; It’s essential not to compound what is already.  I descended far and right as the sun went down and as the weather surprisingly cleared up.&nbsp; While descending through the loose rock I heard and then saw the helicopter flying into the valley below, landing near base camp, a touch and go.&nbsp;</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">When I made it down, soaked from the high grasses, I arrived to a guy standing in fresh mountain apparel and a <em>policia</em> ball-cap with a calm, attentive demeanor scanning the mountainside.&nbsp; He was curious where I had just been.  He offered to warm me some water and fed me some crackers as the helicopter returned with the rest of the team.&nbsp; As I hydrated I watched the team of eight men organize their equipment and pack their bags all by headlamp.&nbsp; Despite the forecast, the weather was now exceptionally clear.&nbsp; I asked the men if they would ascend and one of them looked at me plainly and said “we have to”.&nbsp; I relayed some info to the team lead, and left.&nbsp;</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">As I hiked out of the valley another wave of fierce grief overwhelmed me and I noticed how bright one of the stars were in the night sky.&nbsp; I offerred a prayer to P’s family, whom of course I do not know.&nbsp; I gave a good farewell, relating and rejoicing in the transition from this life.  Another hour of walking I found myself somewhere near my equipment but did not bother to retrieve it and instead found a warm, dry, flat section under a caved out boulder, crawled into my bag and slept soundly until the 4’ o clock shivers came- a routine for the past week.  At twilight I recovered my stove and made tea, enjoying the beautiful morning glow touching Santa Cruz Peak.&nbsp; I finished off my cheesy polenta which was also the remainder of my food as I watched the helicopter make several passes in and out of valley with P’s now discovered body.</p>


  



































  
    
  

    

    

    
