  <p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">It took a while to get packed up again, my left arm was mostly useless and throbbing.&nbsp; Nonetheless I got going and made a stout pace under load.&nbsp; Passing the lake I enjoyed this wonderful interaction with a native guy.&nbsp; He insisted on using more body language than words asking if I would go swimming, teasing me like a child.&nbsp; I teased him back as we shared this peculiar, uplifting exchange.&nbsp;</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">When I arrived to the first established camp after a few hours of walking, I spoke with an <em>arriero</em> and his wife.&nbsp; They were curious about the events unfolding in the valley.&nbsp; I divulged what had transpired in the past 24 hours through what surprised me as remarkably fluent and clear Spanish.&nbsp; The woman met the news of death with humor. The man was gentle and acknowledging. Both of these responses landed with me as natural and appropriate- a deeper understanding was present.</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">This was contrasted by the interaction I then had with a group of European/ American hikers that were standing around waiting for their things to be loaded by this native couple.&nbsp; I didn’t feel organically compelled to divulge much with them but after being offered a cigarette (which I sucked down) I shared a few brief facts. One of the girls remarked “that must be very stressful undergoing what you just did alone”. Touched by her acknowledgment I began to cry again under my heavy pack, witnessed by the familiar serious undertone behind vacant faces.&nbsp; I thanked the situation and walked on, pushing on to the final couple of hours to Cashapampa.&nbsp;</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">&nbsp;Caraz, not far below Cashapampa, is where I am now and it is 330a and I am starting to nod, unworried.&nbsp; Tomorrow I will go to the doctor and enjoy a hearty meal.&nbsp; Today, tomorrow, however it reads, will be a good day.</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal"><br class="ProseMirror-trailingBreak"></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal"><br><strong>Epilogue</strong></p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">I am pro-epilogue in my writing.  Just as most mountaineering stories end with a summit, most stories miss a proper ending.  It’s in the subtlety of post-hype where the greatest lessons are learned, and if were are paying attention these are found well after we’ve left the mountain.</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">My wrist was pretty fucked for few weeks and I needed to immobilize it.  My immune system has also suffered and even after nearly a month elapsing since the climb, I am still sorting through one medical issue after another.  Lately it has been my digestion.</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">A few days after returning to Caraz, a friend showed up in town and we decided to go into the mountains.  With a splint on my wrist and an active chest infection, we decided to ascend into another valley and give a non-technical summit a look.  I had contracted food poisoning and we were delayed a day as I vomitted and rested from vertigo in the tent half-way up the valley.  After summoning some energy to go further we arrived to the refugio which served as a great support to recover.  Knowing that I tend to switch into a different energy at higher elevations, I was not surprised when a wave of newness refreshed me.  What was originally intended to be an afternoon gear stash, reconnaissance hike and sunset photo-shoot with her my friend’s old Nikon, turned out to be a summit push.  We were afforded a clear sky, healthy feelings in our bodies, and stable conditions under our feet.  We made an unconventional summit, putting our crampons on at sunset.  </p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">Within minutes of exiting the mountains the next day, I was notified of something quite shocking and delicate back home.  A few days later, news of friend’s version of it arrived.  </p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class="MsoNormal">Although I have been deeply trialed physically and psycho-emotionally lately, living and dying continues.  But woah, do I feel lucky.  What the mountains graciously give me I get to bring into my life.  Something we all have in us, when we open to it.   </p>


  



































  
    
  

    

    

    
