  <p class=""><strong>Leaving Society</strong>&nbsp;</p><p class="">Screaming down the German autobahn my Audi and I howl.&nbsp; Away from the plastic and shallow acting people call “work”.  Away from the mechanized interactions and pretending people call “the real world”.&nbsp; I am excited for what is to come, excited to be raw.&nbsp; Underneath scared for what is to come; last time I left trembling.  Perhaps that’s why I am returning.&nbsp;</p><p class="">Upon arrival a kind of seduction set in.&nbsp; I met him late evening at a wine bar.&nbsp; There we were, sipping our wine discussing our love affair.&nbsp; For both of us life in the mountains had a newness to it.&nbsp; He was an aspiring guide and I was a skier with a deep interest and curiosity to the life of a climber.&nbsp; As we romanticized the aura of the room took on that which you might find in a late night jazz club.&nbsp; His english was broken, I spoke no french.&nbsp; At some mood the alcohol took away our energy to try to understand one another when he said something both poetic and foretelling- “we don’t have to translate, the mountains will”.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">The next morning was all arranged.&nbsp; As it turns out, his girlfriend works for the famous cable-car of the Aiguille Du Midi which gives us a bypass to the long line of tourists.&nbsp; Suited up I feel a sense of self-importance brushing shoulders with the common tourists in their jeans and peacoats.&nbsp; We aren't going up there to take pretty pictures.&nbsp; I oddly enjoy the conversation shared with a couple of Americans on the cable car, also climbers.  It’s rare for me to be in such a positive mood.  &nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">The altitude gain is over 3000 meters/ 10000 feet in a matter of half an hour. Gawking from the balconies we soon discover how different of a world it is up there.&nbsp; But for us, we aren't up there to take pretty pictures.&nbsp; We take little time lingering as we move through the tunnels making our way to the ice cave; the 20 foot transition zone from insulated modern society to the world of exposure.&nbsp; It feels like the beginning of a rite of passage sitting on the bench as I adjust my crampons; looking around at the skiers, paragliders and other mountaineers.&nbsp; The tunnel exits to a knife edge descent that wraps down and planes out into a glacier at 13,000 feet. From there, it’s an alpine playground.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">That evening we chose to traverse onto some terrain that would justify a couple of rappels to get down. Before dark, we made our way over to the nearby and luxurious alpine hut for a comfortable and acclimatizing night of sleep.  Early the next morning we found our way across a classic traverse, expanding our lungs and communication along the way.  And later that day we connected a system of cable cars to return back to the Chamonix Valley for a juicy burger and a few gear purchases.  With a quick turnaround we load into the girlfriend’s car and drive out of the valley a few hours to begin our objective.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class=""></p><p class=""><strong>More Background</strong>&nbsp;</p><p class="sqsrte-large">Perhaps this is a good time to share some relevant history between Ed and myself.&nbsp; We had met six months prior- he having freshly moved to Chamonix Valley while I and partner at the time were on a weekend ski trip.&nbsp; While skiing together the next day our passions met.&nbsp; We stayed in touch and a couple months later I returned to the Valley for a week of adventure with him.&nbsp; Over the course of a week we climbed and skied several moderate classics.&nbsp; What we lacked in technical knowledge and experience we made up for with a cavalier attitude.&nbsp; It was clear almost immediately that we were both at an exceptional level of fitness with high risk thresholds.&nbsp; We assumed that whatever the mountains would throw at us, we would figure it out as it came.&nbsp; As we moved through each up and down that week it would slowly reveal just how much we were willing to put out.&nbsp; Our last objective of that trip was a debacle.&nbsp; With each rappel our lack of experience raised eyebrows. In hind-sight moments beyond my conscious control saved me.&nbsp; We took risk after risk crossing the glacier to barely miss the last cable-car back down to the valley.&nbsp; This meant that we would need to spend the night in the Du Midi station, which by late afternoon was already below freezing.&nbsp; We spent our last night together waking each other up to do push-ups and air squats.&nbsp; We were curled up around a toilet in the woman’s bathroom.&nbsp; It was the warmest place we could find.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p>


  



































  
    
  

    

    

    
