  <p class=""><strong>The Approach</strong>&nbsp;</p><p class="">Reaching the trail head at dark, it’s my turn to carry the rope.  Ed had held this burden earlier today .  Two hours in I am dry heaving and I don’t want to admit I’m already cramping, much less my pounding skull.  “Fucking idiot.&nbsp; Why are you carrying water Landon?&nbsp; There’s a river right there”, I am thinking.  &nbsp;</p><p class="">He says: “You just have to accept to suffer”  I nod, and laugh.  &nbsp;</p><p class="">“I’ve barely begun”, I am thinking.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">Finally, we reach the first of several unmanned huts this route offers.&nbsp; At 8,000ft. it is relatively low in altitude, but it’s clear I am sick from the altitude.&nbsp; I force down some food and the warm tea calms me down.  Before long, I pass out into a deep sleep.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">The next day is different.  Our task is to navigate semi-technical terrain and gain some elevation toward the end.  We pass through a period of bickering at one another as a rhythm works it's way into the labor we're performing.  We arrive at the second hut with good timing just before losing day-light and visibility from fog.&nbsp; We prioritize drinking, eating and drinking more.  Just past sunset, two guys enter.&nbsp; We all realize we’re going for the same route and decide to join forces.&nbsp; Not long after going to sleep a fifth guy enters the hut well past twilight- a paraglider.&nbsp;  &nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">At midnight we follow the other two in the dark trudging through deeper snow conditions than any of us had expected.&nbsp; At some places it is waist deep. Maybe we are drifting off route?  A few hours pass and after ascending a steep snow slope we find our first section of rock.&nbsp; From the distant belay I have the luxury of a broader view.  I look down&nbsp; and see lights from an Italian village.&nbsp; It looks like a little cluster directly below, surrounded by a vastness of blackness.  I marvel over at the three french guys.  Their carelessness and ease with the exposure, their laughter.  I am intrigued by the one climber who is taking a risky detour off route. With their backs fully exposed one guy is belaying off of a slung flake while the other hacks away with his tools trying to get a passive piece of pro in, slightly inverted.&nbsp; Ed is patiently waiting to see if it will go, conversing with the belayer.&nbsp; After a few falls the leader comes down and shakes his head.&nbsp; I feel like I am standing on an edge of threshold.&nbsp; I'm well read and inspired, but not yet initiated.&nbsp; They point their heads back on route.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">Climb, traverse, climb, traverse, climb.  Light is appearing in the sky as we weave over and through the rocks. The fluidity of the movement brings a smile to my face.&nbsp; No different than how I felt as a young boy weaving my way up a tree.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">We sit under a wind lip near the summit just after sunrise.&nbsp; We take a short break and mentally prepare ourselves for the descent, which is more like a long traverse... part two.  &nbsp;</p><p class="">&nbsp;</p><p class="">*The route we are on is on the list of big-route objectives for prospective guides to tick off.&nbsp; Their certification requires completing a half-dozen of these climbs at this commitment level and difficulty.&nbsp; Ed, having recently abandoned his life in Paris in pursuit of another life in the mountains needed someone to ‘practice guide’.&nbsp; This relationship was mutually beneficial.&nbsp; He got to practice, I got a fast-pass into another extreme endeavor.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">&nbsp;</p><p class="">The descent is actually the allure of this objective.&nbsp; It’s a sustained snowy knife edge with sheer drops on either side, making an arrest nearly impossible.&nbsp; This balance beam demands focusing one foot in front of the other.  Connected by a section of rope, the agreement was that if one fell off on one-side the other would jump the opposite.&nbsp; As the morning warmed up our fitness and recent nights at higher elevation begins revealing it's magic, we cover a great deal of snow and rock in short period of time.&nbsp; We are feeling confident.  Managing something bigger than originally intended is becoming plausible, as we sit in a little rock out-cropping looking up toward Mont Blanc proper.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">“There is a cloud approaching, you see?”&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">“Yeah, I see”.&nbsp;</p><p class="">The other two guys had fallen back and as the weather begins to turn we see them change course as Ed and I ascend. Fast.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">&nbsp;</p><p class="">&nbsp;</p><p class=""><strong>The Storm</strong>&nbsp;</p><p class="">The wind picks up and everything becomes increasingly white.  We check our altimeters and stand with our backs to the wind, huddled close so we can hear each other.  We both verbalize our gut feeling to turn towards the direction to the hut; yet, the irrational excitement for unknown mixed with our invincible attitudes we decide to continue up even more.  The storm strengthens as I feel it coming on.  I am about to suffer, and know it.&nbsp; Pretty soon things will become darker, colder, and louder than they already are.  As the wind whips and we merge into the storm small frozen pellets pound against my eyeballs.&nbsp; TOOL begins to play inside my head.&nbsp; The heavy instrumentals, the fast twitching of the drums and the mysterious lyrics are fitting as we move in a stir of confusion. I smile.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">&nbsp;</p>


  



































  
    
  

    

    

    
