  <p class="">*Our altimeters say we have plateaued.  We check it off as a mental success leaving us with the reality of where we are. We hurry in the direction we think we should return back to safety.&nbsp; Fact was, where we thought we were going is not where we were going.&nbsp; A different story had been written out for us this day.  The way we are going will reveal the consequences for the choices we have made as we unknowingly descend into a nightmare.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">&nbsp;</p><p class="">My head is racing. “Which way?  Are you sure this way?  No, no, no, no this is wrong!  Fuck! Fuck you!” The terrain steepens and the pain from the cramping in my muscles become secondary to the whipping cold.  Six months ago my hands got cold and I came away with a frostbitten pinky.&nbsp; Now my hands are really cold.&nbsp; Ed’s tugs from the rope urging me to follow what he thinks might be the way out.  Indecision descends into my body paralyzing me only to feel another tug from the rope.  What I despise in humans I have now become.&nbsp; A sheep on a leash.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">For hours we are kicking and poking our way in all directions inside of this ping pong ball.&nbsp; With some kicks, nothing. Slowly I begin to realize that this can actually kill me.&nbsp; It registers that this could be the last thing I see:&nbsp; white.&nbsp; The blind faith that had carried me this far ebbs into a stir of rage and desperation, as my hope diminishes.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">The struggle inside comes to a calm as a small voice makes itself known.&nbsp;&nbsp;<br><br></p><p class=""><br></p><p class=""><span class="sqsrte-text-color--white"><strong>No Man’s Land</strong></span>&nbsp;</p><p class=""><br></p><p class=""><br></p><p class=""><span class="sqsrte-text-color--white">I don’t know if this moment was eternal or instantaneous; if we had descended beneath the storm or the storm had lifted above us?</span>  <span class="sqsrte-text-color--white">Perhaps both and all of it.</span>&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class=""><span class="sqsrte-text-color--white">Before I knew it, there we were standing on a snow slope looking down into a virgin world.&nbsp; Vividly bright, never before in my life had I seen such purity and beauty as what lay ahead.&nbsp; The vastness of such a landscape was much like a lucid dream.&nbsp; On distant sides were massive mountainous walls- the one to our left frozen and wintry looking, the one to our right actively falling apart creating a symphony of sounds while being baked in the mid-day sun.&nbsp; Below our feet seracs, crevasses, ice ramps and snow slopes all intertwined and spiraled downward thousands of feet into a blind drop-off that looked like an abyss.&nbsp; To our backs was where we had come from.&nbsp; Far, far away in what felt like a distant world was civilization. We were in no-man’s land.&nbsp;</span></p><p class=""><span class="sqsrte-text-color--white">To say that I was exhausted is an understatement.&nbsp; We had hardly eaten since midnight, drank very little water, and all four of my limbs had seized up from cramps hours ago.&nbsp; Not only was the terrain itself beyond my limits of experience, but so to was the physical state my body was in.&nbsp; Had Ed not protected what he did, I would not be alive.&nbsp; After one fall I took, I don’t know what made me dry heave- realizing that I had just fallen or that I was alive.&nbsp; The next few hours would continually evoke far beyond what I believed a human could do only to give way to another state of horror and exhaustion then morphing into further creativity.&nbsp; Such is the wonder of survival.&nbsp; The spontaneous moments of gazing into each other’s eyes exchanging both the terror and joy of the experience speaks to one extreme.  Realizing that this is what it feels like to be edging with death, realizing how of an alone encounter that is, that moment speaks to another extreme.  </span></p>


  



































  
    
  

    

    

    
