  <p class="">&nbsp;</p><p class="">After some hours we reached another threshold.&nbsp; Standing on some unstable rock still uncertain as to what was below us when suddenly out of my mouth slipped the words I vowed never to say- “I quit”.&nbsp; He asked if I was truly done and I tried to undo it but didn’t have the strength.&nbsp; Yes, I had had enough.&nbsp; Not that it really meant anything because who would save us?&nbsp;</p><p class="">We stood there for a moment, balancing.&nbsp; Then Ed started digging into his pack, pulling out his cell phone.&nbsp; I asked what he was doing and he said that he was going to try to call rescue.&nbsp; It hadn’t occurred to me that this was even an option.&nbsp; His cell phone was dead.&nbsp; We stood there for a moment, balancing.&nbsp; Then Ed remembered something, he had brought an external charger.&nbsp; It worked, and he made the call.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">As he stood there <em>parle’ing </em>in French I felt some hope returning.&nbsp; He got off the phone and relayed the news.&nbsp; The dispatch said that they could send out a helicopter to try and find us but with it being later in the afternoon and in such an obscure area for us not to expect a rescue to happen today.&nbsp; I asked Ed what that meant, if they couldn’t find us and he drily said “we’ll spend the night here”.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">&nbsp;</p><p class="">*Aforementioned, I had spent the previous summer going through some challenging experiences, mostly with the military.&nbsp; One of those assessments was a two week Army Ranger assessment.&nbsp; Our last patrol was ex-filtration by helicopter.&nbsp; Weeks later I attended Survival School with the Air Force.&nbsp; In the middle of our six-day survival period our team practiced a field rescue, by helicopter.&nbsp;</p><p class=""><br></p><p class="">Not long after sending over our coordinates we heard a faint noise down the valley.&nbsp; I pulled out an emergency blanket in hopes of flagging them down.&nbsp; Within a minute that little dot came moving up the swath passing us by at a distance only to loop around at the top of the glacier and come back downward... passing by us again only to disappear.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">&nbsp;</p><p class="">(I hope by now you’re the seeing both the irony and the humor in this story).&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">&nbsp;</p><p class="">&nbsp;</p><p class=""><strong>The Scenic Way Down</strong>&nbsp;</p><p class="">After a long moment of quiet, the helicopter appears again this time and passes by closer as it loops lower and comes toward us.&nbsp; I knew by now they had spotted us.&nbsp; Very soon a man lowered by a steel cable would come swinging into us.&nbsp; It was a swift rescue plucking Ed off first leaving me alone for a few moments of quiet to look back into all that had been passed through.&nbsp; Moments later the sound of the helicopter chopped through the silence.&nbsp; He quickly came swinging in, clipped my harness and we accelerated away from the mountain-side.&nbsp; The onrush of wind turbulence was exhilarating and as we cut through the air.&nbsp; This good man asked if I was okay.&nbsp; I motioned with a nod that I was, physically speaking.&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">Our “fast-passes” were running dry.&nbsp; To my surprise they dropped us off not in town but rather at a station three hours hike up trail.&nbsp; Ed asked for my basic information and took it to the helicopter crew.&nbsp; While there I noticed the crew's excitation by what had just happened, repeating something in French.&nbsp; When he returned he said that I had just exhausted my one free helicopter ride on the municipals dime (he had already used his months prior). I asked what everyone was laughing about.&nbsp; The crew told him that we were the first live bodies they had recovered from that glacier- <em>première descente</em>.&nbsp;</p><p class="">We were quiet most of the walk down.&nbsp; The flushed heat in my face had a lot to say, but no words could leave my parched mouth.&nbsp; Off the trail Ed’s kind girlfriend met us roadside with her vehicle and a coke.&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;</p><p class="">&nbsp;</p><p class=""><strong>Epilogue</strong>&nbsp;</p><p class="">This ordeal has been both a mark of tremendous pain in my life as well as tremendous gift to my life. Seven years having elapsed almost to the week I am re-reading it.  The transformation of such an experience is built into how life reveals itself to me.  It’s not until the past few years that I can weather a thunderstorm without being overwhelmed with an imminency of death. The shaking inside is a way of saying “hello”.&nbsp;It wasn’t until two fall-times ago that I gained the courage to cross a small glacier.&nbsp; The water carving it’s way through the ice brought me to tears.&nbsp; &nbsp;</p><p class="">It would be ideal to sit here and tell you that coming off that mountain that day would be the beginning of beautiful and gentle healing and yes I get more of that these days.  Initially, it was stacked into a whole lot more.&nbsp; I was no stranger to taking risks with my life and finding myself in horrid conditions before this happened (and not just in the mountains).&nbsp; More would continue afterward.&nbsp; Yet, little did I know, and less do I know now, that my life turns in such a way both to preserve me as well as humble me.  Being back on a glacier for a long, longer, and very long day the experience moves through such a depth I cannot, and won’t try to, describe.  A world transformed is to see our Self in the world.  And my goodness to just turn for a moment, and see just how beautiful ‘it’ worthy of a closer look, wherever it is, even in those moments that yeah make one side truly mysterious, and yet in another seeing Landon, you’re just being Landon.  </p><p class=""> Slowly through descent can see just a bit wider.  </p><p data-rte-preserve-empty="true" class=""></p><p class=""><br></p><p class=""><span class="sqsrte-text-color--lightAccent">If you read this and you and I have shared a rope, I want to thank you.  Let’s do it again sometime, yeah?  And if we haven’t or may never, albeit the story can be exclusive, I want to give you an even warmer thank you.  As in the words of Maurice Herzog-  “there are other mountains in the lives of wo/men.</span></p>


  



































  
    
  

    

    

    
